[1mNAME[0m
       write - send a message to another user

       [1mwrite [4m[22muser[24m [[4mttyname[24m]

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       [1mwrite [22mallows you to communicate with other users, by copying lines from your
       terminal to theirs.

       When you run the [1mwrite [22mcommand, the user you are writing to gets a message of the
       form:

           Message from yourname@yourhost on yourtty at hh:mm ...

       Any further lines you enter will be copied to the specified user’s terminal. If the
       other user wants to reply, they must run [1mwrite [22mas well.

       When you are done, type an end-of-file or interrupt character. The other user will
       see the message [1mEOF [22mindicating that the conversation is over.

       You can prevent people (other than the superuser) from writing to you with the
       [1mmesg[22m(1) command. Some commands, for example [1mnroff[22m(1) and [1mpr[22m(1), may automatically
       disallow writing, so that the output they produce isn’t overwritten.

       If the user you want to write to is logged in on more than one terminal, you can
       specify which terminal to write to by giving the terminal name as the second operand
       to the [1mwrite [22mcommand. Alternatively, you can let [1mwrite [22mselect one of the terminals -
       it will pick the one with the shortest idle time. This is so that if the user is
       logged in at work and also dialed up from home, the message will go to the right
       place.

       The traditional protocol for writing to someone is that the string [4m-o[24m, either at the
       end of a line or on a line by itself, means that it’s the other person’s turn to
       talk. The string [4moo[24m means that the person believes the conversation to be over.

